tests/fixtures/video265/ -- a committed HEVC stream and the CRC32 of its
correct decode.

sample.h265  Main profile, 8-bit 4:2:0, 176x144, 21 pictures, I and P slices
             only, keyint 12 so there are two IRAPs and the DPB turns over.
             Encoded with ffmpeg/libx265 4.1 at qp 27 with deterministic
             settings (pools=none:frame-threads=1:wpp=0:rc-lookahead=0).
sample.crc32 CRC32 of the concatenated visible YUV of all 21 decoded pictures
             in output order -- the number tests/unit/h265test.c prints with no
             reference argument, and the number /bin/vidcheck265 prints from
             inside LogitOS.

Why this is committed rather than generated: `make test-h265` builds its main
matrix with ffmpeg/libx265, which is not installed everywhere. Without a
vendored stream that target would silently become a no-op on such a machine.
Same reasoning and same arrangement as tests/fixtures/video for H.264.

The CRC was pinned from a decode verified BIT-EXACT against ffmpeg's own HEVC
decoder -- h265_test sample.h265 sample.ref.yuv printed "21 pictures
bit-exact" -- so it is a reference value, not a number this decoder recorded
about itself.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
main10.h265  The ITU/JCT-VC conformance bitstream WP_A_MAIN10_Toshiba_3, from
             the official draft_conformance set. Main 10, 10-bit 4:2:0,
             416x240, 256 pictures, weighted sample prediction for P slices
             with plural reference indices. main10.txt is the description
             file shipped in the conformance package, unmodified.
main10.crc32 CRC32 of the concatenated visible samples of all 256 decoded
             pictures at NATIVE PRECISION (two bytes per sample, little
             endian) -- the "H265-CRC16" number h265test.c prints for a
             stream deeper than 8 bits.

This one is worth more than the generated matrix and more than sample.h265,
for one reason: its decode was checked against the MD5 the conformance
package ships, not against ffmpeg. Dumping our 256 decoded pictures in HM's
raw layout gives

    c48e853010a234786dc87790d053b03a

which is byte for byte the value in WP_A_MAIN10_Toshiba_3_yuv.md5. Matching
ffmpeg says two independent decoders agree; matching this says we agree with
the reference decoder the format is defined by. The CRC32 pinned here is a
hash of exactly those bytes, so gating on it is gating on that MD5 without
committing a 200 MB YUV file.

Three other Main 10 conformance streams were run and are NOT committed,
because the decoder does not pass them and a fixture that is expected to fail
is just a comment:
  DBLK_A_MAIN10_VIXS_4     uses PCM, which this decoder refuses outright
  INITQP_B_Main10_Sony_1   B slices
  WPP_A_ericsson_MAIN10_2  B slices
